[Student "S"]
{You need to find out if partner has 4 \Ss. Using lebensohl the way to do that is to first bid 2NT. Partner bids 2\C, the relay bid. What do say next? 

You need to find out if partner has 4 \Ss. Using lebensohl the way to do that is to first bid 2NT. Partner bids 2\C, the relay bid. What do say next?

Now you cue-bid 3\H. An immediate cue-bid would have been Stayman, (for the other Major), but the cue-bid following the lebensohl 2NT also promises a stopper in the enemy suit. In any case, partner replies 3\S. Next? 

You need to find out if partner has 4 \Ss. Using lebensohl the way to do that is to first bid 2NT. Partner bids 2\C, the relay bid. What do say next?

Now you cue-bid 3\H. An immediate cue-bid would have been Stayman, (for the other Major), but the cue-bid following the lebensohl 2NT also promises a stopper in the enemy suit. In any case, partner replies 3\S. Next?

You bid 4\S.

The contract would be 4\S played by East.

West plays 4\S. North leads the \HK. Do you take the first trick or not?

Loser List: \S = 1/2 : \H = 1 : \D = 1 : \C = 0 :: Total = 3/4

You should not hold up on the \H for two reasons. First, South might have a singleton. Second, you might not have a \H loser at all (if you can discard both of dummy's \H losers on your \Cs. 

So win the \HA and start on trumps by playing \SA, then \SK. Uh Oh. South discards a \H on the second \S so you have two \S losers. 

Now you will have to be lucky. Leave the two master trumps undrawn and start running the \Cs. Fortunately South has 4 \Cs and you are able to discard both of dummy's \H losers. Play a \D next and your luck continues when it is North who takes the trick. Since South can't get in to pull your trumps, you cannot be defeated. You play your \D winners and ruff a \H in dummy. South will get his two trumps at the end.

Imagine what would have happened if you had played a third trump.

South would have taken the two trump tricks, then North would have taken 5 \H tricks and the \DA.}
[Play "N"]
HK

